add('P52A-CPI-M-005','A fixed basket has two meals at a base price of $4 each and one fare at $12. Meal prices rise 10%; the fare is unchanged. What CPI results, with the base set to 100?',
 ['104; meals had 40% of base basket spending','106.7; meals were two-thirds of the units','110; use the price change of the only item that rose','140; the meal expenditure weight is the inflation rate'],
 'Base cost is 2 × 4 + 12 = 20. Current cost is 2 × 4.40 + 12 = 20.80, giving CPI 104. The relevant weight is the 8/20 spending share, not the two-of-three item count.','B: correct the false inference from physical units to expenditure weights.', [('base cost','2*4+12',20),('current cost','2*4.4+12',20.8),('CPI','20.8/20*100',104)])
add('P52A-CPI-M-002','An index moves from 175 to 189. A report calls the 14-point rise “14 percent inflation.” Which correction uses the proper comparison?',
 ['8%, because the 14-point rise is divided by 175','7.4%, because the 14-point rise is divided by 189','14%, because one index point is one percent','89%, because every comparison must begin at 100'],
 'Inflation is the change relative to the previous index: (189 - 175)/175 × 100 = 8%. Index points and percentage changes are different.','B: replace an exact legacy match to the simple exam item with denominator-error diagnosis.', [('inflation','(189-175)/175*100',8)])
add(nl('ELITE-321'),'A basket costs $750 when the CPI is 125. One year later the same basket costs $810. Which new index and claim about inflation are correct?',
 ['CPI 135; inflation is 8%, although the index rose 10 points','CPI 108; inflation is 8%, because the old index resets each year','CPI 135; inflation is 10%, matching the index-point increase','CPI 162; inflation is 35%, measured from the base year'],
 'The unchanged base-basket cost is 750/1.25 = 600. The new index is 810/600 × 100 = 135. The 10-point increase is 10/125 = 8%, not 10%.','C/D: reverse the basket base and connect the new index to inflation.', [('base','750/1.25',600),('CPI','810/600*100',135),('inflation','(810/750-1)*100',8)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9023'),'A fixed basket contains 3 tickets and 2 repairs. Base prices are $8 and $18. Last year the basket cost $72. This year its CPI is 135 and each ticket costs $9. What repair price and annual inflation rate are implied?',
 ['$27 per repair and 12.5% inflation','$27 per repair and 35% inflation','$24 per repair and 12.5% inflation','$40.50 per repair and 35% inflation'],
 'Base cost is 3 × 8 + 2 × 18 = 60. CPI 135 implies current cost 81. After ticket spending of 27, the two repairs cost 54, or 27 each. Inflation since last year is (81 - 72)/72 = 12.5%.','C/D: reconstruct a missing price and chain the basket index to annual inflation.', [('base','3*8+2*18',60),('repair price','(60*1.35-3*9)/2',27),('inflation','(81/72-1)*100',12.5)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9024'),'An index is rebased: the old series lists last year at 160 and this year at 176, while the new series sets last year to 100. A nominal benefit rose 6% over the period. Which comparison survives rebasing?',
 ['The new current index is 110; the benefit lost purchasing power','The new current index is 116; the benefit lost purchasing power','The new current index is 110; the benefit gained 6% in real terms','The new current index is 100; rebasing removes inflation'],
 'Scaling both indexes by 100/160 makes the new current index 110. Inflation remains 10%; a 6% nominal increase is smaller, so real purchasing power falls. Changing the reference base cannot erase price growth.','C/D: distinguish index rebasing from inflation and evaluate a real payment.', [('rebased index','176/160*100',110),('inflation','(176/160-1)*100',10)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9025'),'A basket contains 4 units of A at a base price of $6 and an unknown quantity of B at $12. Its base cost is $72. Current prices are $9 for A and $15 for B. Which CPI and next-period inflation follow if the same basket then costs $100.80?',
 ['CPI 133.3; next-period inflation 5%','CPI 140; next-period inflation 40%','CPI 133.3; next-period inflation 40%','CPI 125; next-period inflation 12%'],
 'B accounts for 72 - 24 = 48 base dollars, so there are 4 units. Current cost is 4 × 9 + 4 × 15 = 96 and CPI ≈ 133.3. The next increase is (100.80 - 96)/96 = 5%.','C/D: infer basket quantity before index and inflation calculations.', [('B quantity','(72-4*6)/12',4),('current cost','4*9+4*15',96),('next inflation','(100.8/96-1)*100',5)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9026'),'A nominal hourly wage rises from $25 to $27. Basket cost rises from $500 to $540, then falls to $525 while the wage stays $27. Relative to the starting year, what real-wage change remains after both price movements?',
 ['About 2.9% higher; the first raise just matched prices, then prices partly fell','8% higher; only the nominal wage change matters','Unchanged; the first year’s full indexation guarantees this forever','About 2.9% lower; a later price decline reduces purchasing power'],
 'The cumulative wage factor is 27/25 = 1.08 and cumulative price factor is 525/500 = 1.05. Their ratio is 1.02857, giving about 2.9% more purchasing power. Use cumulative levels instead of adding annual rates.','C/D: multi-period real-wage interpretation with an intervening price reversal.', [('real growth','((27/25)/(525/500)-1)*100',2.857142857142857)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9027'),'A fixed basket’s cost rises 20% in one year and falls 10% in the next. A commentator claims prices are back where they started because growth has reversed. Which cumulative index and purchasing-power statement are correct, starting from 100?',
 ['Index 108; unchanged dollars buy less than initially','Index 110; subtract annual rates to obtain the exact total','Index 100; opposite price changes cancel','Index 90; only the most recent inflation rate matters'],
 'The cumulative factor is 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.08. Prices remain 8% above the starting level, so unchanged nominal income has less purchasing power. A decline does not undo an earlier rise of a different percentage base.','C/D: compound inflation and evaluate a misleading reversal claim.', [('final index','100*1.2*.9',108)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9028'),'A basket agency sees households replacing a newly expensive rail route with an existing bus route. A trial finds the cheaper travel bundle preserves the same commuting service. Which evidence supports substitution bias, and what does it fail to establish?',
 ['A fixed bundle overstates the cost of that service; the evidence does not quantify bias for the whole CPI','The bus is a new good; the evidence measures all economy-wide quality gains','The rail price rise is entirely a quality change; no pure inflation occurred','The change proves the whole CPI should fall because one alternative is cheaper'],
 'The same service can be maintained at lower cost by changing an existing bundle, which is the substitution mechanism. A travel example alone does not measure the full index’s bias or imply that overall prices fell.','C: evaluate equivalence evidence and inference limits rather than identify a label.')
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9029'),'A new pump costs 12% more than the old model and lasts 12% longer with identical annual performance and maintenance costs. Ignoring discounting, which conclusion is supported for this product, and which is not?',
 ['Cost per service-year is unchanged; economy-wide inflation cannot be inferred','Cost per service-year rises 12%; durability is irrelevant to quality','Cost per service-year falls 12%; equal percentage changes must be subtracted from price twice','Economy-wide inflation is zero because this product has no service-cost rise'],
 'Price per lifetime service-year changes by 1.12/1.12 = 1. The sticker-price rise alone is therefore not pure service-cost inflation for this product. Its example cannot establish the economy-wide price change.','C/D: quantify a controlled quality comparison and limit its interpretation.', [('service cost ratio','1.12/1.12',1)])
add('PM2B2-BIAS-L-001','A fixed basket costs 9% more, while a redesigned bundle costs only 3% more. Before concluding that substitution bias is six percentage points, what must an analyst establish?',
 ['The redesigned bundle preserves the relevant living standard, with comparable quality and coverage','Households bought fewer total items, regardless of the services they lost','Both bundles contain the same number of product names, regardless of quantities','The redesigned bundle is cheaper, even if it provides less value'],
 'A cheaper bundle can reflect reduced consumption or quality rather than cost-saving substitution at the same living standard. The comparison must hold the relevant standard of living constant before interpreting the gap as bias.','C: evaluate the missing identifying condition in a plausible bias calculation.')
add('PM2B2-DEF-H-002','Between two years, individual prices do not change, but domestic production shifts toward goods whose current prices are higher relative to their base-year prices. Why can a fixed-base GDP deflator rise?',
 ['Current quantities put more weight on goods with larger price increases since the base year','The deflator must be unchanged whenever current-year prices are unchanged','The deflator begins including imported consumer goods as domestic output','A shift toward goods with higher dollar prices raises every index regardless of base prices'],
 'The deflator compares current-price and base-price values of current quantities. Changing the output mix toward larger current-to-base price relatives can raise the ratio even with no new individual price changes. High dollar prices alone do not establish that result.','B: clarify the ambiguous meaning of relative prices; preserve composition skill.')
add(nl('ELITE-331'),'A government must update a household allowance and also convert nominal GDP to real GDP. Imported household energy gets dearer while domestic investment equipment gets cheaper. Which pairing and interpretation fit?',
 ['Use CPI for the allowance and the GDP deflator for GDP; the indexes can move differently','Use the GDP deflator for both because energy purchases reduce net exports','Use CPI for both because every domestic output price is in its basket','Use either index for either task because all price changes have equal weights'],
 'The household payment needs a consumer-price measure, which includes imported consumer energy. Deflating GDP requires prices of domestic production, including investment goods. Their differing coverage permits divergent movements.','C: apply index choice to competing household and production tasks.')
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9032'),'An economy’s consumer basket has base spending of $40 on imported tea and $60 on domestic services. Tea prices rise 25%; services prices stay fixed. The only other domestic output is exported machinery, whose price falls 10%. With quantities fixed, which conclusion is justified?',
 ['CPI rises 10%; the GDP deflator falls, but its size needs domestic-output weights','Both indexes rise 25% because imports are in consumer spending','CPI rises 25%; the GDP deflator falls exactly 10%','CPI is unchanged; imported consumer prices are excluded'],
 'The consumer basket rises from 100 to 40 × 1.25 + 60 = 110, a 10% increase. The domestic deflator includes unchanged service prices and lower machinery prices, so it falls; their output weights are needed to measure the decline.','C/D: calculate one index while recognizing information insufficient for another.', [('CPI growth','(40*1.25+60)/(40+60)*100-100',10)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9033'),'Domestic output consists of 10 machines and 100 services. Base prices are $100 and $10; current prices are $120 and $10. Consumers buy the services and imported clothing; the clothing price is unchanged. Which price-index account is supported?',
 ['GDP deflator 110; CPI unchanged if its fixed basket contains only those consumer purchases','GDP deflator 120; CPI rises 20% because domestic machines got dearer','GDP deflator 110; CPI must rise by the same 10%','GDP deflator 100; business equipment is not final domestic output'],
 'Base-price domestic output is 10 × 100 + 100 × 10 = 2,000; current-price output is 2,200, giving deflator 110. The stated consumer prices did not change, so their fixed basket’s CPI is unchanged.','C/D: quantify domestic-output weighting and distinguish consumer coverage.', [('deflator','(10*120+100*10)/(10*100+100*10)*100',110)])
add('PM2B2-DEF-L-002','A household grant rises from $2,000 to $2,160 as CPI moves from 125 to 135. Nominal GDP rises 8% while the GDP deflator rises 4%. Which combined assessment uses the appropriate indexes?',
 ['The grant preserves purchasing power; real GDP rises about 3.8%','The grant gains 8% purchasing power; real GDP rises 8%','The grant loses purchasing power; real GDP is unchanged','Both real changes are zero because each nominal value rose 8%'],
 'The grant and consumer-price factors are both 1.08, preserving purchasing power. GDP requires its own price factor: 1.08/1.04 - 1 ≈ 3.8%. Matching nominal rates do not imply matching real changes with different deflators.','C/D: integrate index choice and real-value comparisons.', [('grant ratio','(2160/2000)/(135/125)',1),('GDP growth','(1.08/1.04-1)*100',3.846153846153846)])
add(nl('ELITE-333'),'A stipend pays $1,800 at CPI 150. At CPI 165 it rises to $1,920. How much additional monthly pay would restore the original purchasing power, and why?',
 ['$60; full indexation requires $1,980','$180; the CPI increased by 10%, so add 10% again','$120; any increase exactly restores purchasing power','$0; the stipend already rose in dollars'],
 'Maintaining the initial purchasing power requires 1,800 × 165/150 = 1,980. The actual 1,920 is short by 60. A positive nominal raise may still fall short of price growth.','C/D: convert and compare against an actual partial adjustment.', [('target','1800*165/150',1980),('shortfall','1980-1920',60)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9035'),'A grant is $2,400 at CPI 120. It is fully adjusted when CPI reaches 132, then remains frozen as CPI reaches 144. What final grant and additional payment would restore the starting purchasing power?',
 ['$2,640 paid; an additional $240 is needed','$2,880 paid; no additional payment is needed','$2,640 paid; an additional $264 is needed','$2,400 paid; an additional $480 is needed'],
 'The first adjustment is 2,400 × 132/120 = 2,640. At the final index, preserving starting purchasing power requires 2,400 × 144/120 = 2,880. The frozen payment is short by 240.','C/D: multi-period indexation with a missed adjustment.', [('paid','2400*132/120',2640),('topup','2400*144/120-2640',240)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9036'),'An indexed payment was $900 at CPI 100. Its purchasing power is unchanged at a later $1,260 payment, but the later CPI was lost. The next payment is $1,350 at CPI 150. Which reconstruction and comparison are correct?',
 ['Missing CPI 140; both later payments preserve the initial purchasing power','Missing CPI 126; the $1,350 payment buys 50% more','Missing CPI 140; the $1,350 payment buys 7.1% more in real terms','Missing CPI 150; the two nominal payments have equal dollar value'],
 'Equal purchasing power requires the lost index to be 100 × 1,260/900 = 140. The next payment deflates to 1,350/1.50 = 900 in base dollars, also preserving the initial purchasing power.','C/D: reverse one index and evaluate a subsequent adjustment.', [('missing CPI','100*1260/900',140),('final base value','1350/1.5',900)])
add('PM2B2-INDEX-L-003','A threshold is $40,000 at CPI 100. The contract adjusts next year using the previous year’s inflation. CPI rises to 110, then 121; by the second year the threshold has received only the first 10% adjustment. What is the shortfall from full current indexation?',
 ['$4,400; the paid threshold is $44,000 instead of $48,400','$4,000; apply the second 10% only to the original threshold','$8,400; the threshold never received any adjustment','$0; a one-year lag cannot alter purchasing power'],
 'Full current indexation requires 40,000 × 121/100 = 48,400. The lagged threshold is 40,000 × 1.10 = 44,000. The gap is 4,400; successive inflation rates apply to changing price levels.','C/D: quantify lagged indexation and cumulative price growth.', [('full target','40000*1.21',48400),('lagged','40000*1.1',44000),('shortfall','48400-44000',4400)])
add('PM2B2-RNI-H-003','A loan’s fixed nominal rate is 9%. During its year, CPI rises from 200 to 212. Which conclusion is supported by these observations without a theory of how future loan rates are set?',
 ['The realized real return is approximately 3%','Next year’s nominal rate must rise to 15%','Expected inflation at signing must have been 6%','The realized real return is approximately 15%'],
 'Observed inflation is (212 - 200)/200 = 6%, so the approximate realized real return is 9 - 6 = 3%. These observations do not reveal prior expectations or determine future nominal rates.','B/D: replace internal F2/F6 taxonomy with realized-return inference within the same boundary skill.', [('inflation','(212/200-1)*100',6),('real','9-6',3)])
add('PM2B2-RNI-FB-006','A bank pays a fixed 4% nominal rate. CPI inflation during the year is 6%, but no inflation forecast was recorded at signing. What can be established using the Principles approximation?',
 ['The realized real return is -2%; the expected real return cannot be recovered','Both expected and realized real returns were -2%','The realized real return is 10%; the expected rate is unknown','The nominal contract must have reset to 6% after inflation was observed'],
 'The realized real return uses actual inflation: 4 - 6 = -2%. The expected return needs expected inflation, which was not supplied. A fixed contract does not automatically reset after an inflation surprise.','B/D: remove implementation labels and preserve expected/realized measurement boundary.', [('real','4-6',-2)])
add(nl('ELITE-338'),'A lender expected a 2% real return on a fixed 7% nominal loan. Actual inflation was 2 percentage points higher than expected. Which inflation and realized real rate follow using the approximation?',
 ['7% actual inflation and 0% realized real return','5% actual inflation and 2% realized real return','9% actual inflation and -2% realized real return','7% actual inflation and 4% realized real return'],
 'Expected inflation was 7 - 2 = 5%. Actual inflation was 7%, leaving realized real return 7 - 7 = 0%. The two-point surprise lowers the realized real rate by two points.','C/D: infer missing expectations and apply the surprise.', [('expected inflation','7-2',5),('actual inflation','5+2',7),('real','7-7',0)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9038'),'A lender charged a fixed 8% nominal rate to target a 3% expected real return. CPI subsequently moved from 160 to 172.8. What realized real rate and forecast error follow using approximate real interest?',
 ['0% realized real return; inflation was 3 percentage points above expected','3% realized real return; the target guarantees the outcome','-4.8% realized real return; the CPI-point increase is inflation','5% realized real return; actual inflation was below expected'],
 'The target implies expected inflation of 8 - 3 = 5%. Observed inflation is (172.8 - 160)/160 = 8%, so realized real interest is 0%. Inflation exceeded the expectation by 3 percentage points.','C/D: reverse expected inflation, measure actual inflation and reconcile the return.', [('expected inflation','8-3',5),('actual inflation','(172.8/160-1)*100',8),('real','8-8',0),('error','8-5',3)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9039'),'Two one-year accounts each credit 6% nominal interest. Account A’s year has basket costs moving from $400 to $420; B’s year moves from $500 to $540. Which real-return comparison is valid using the approximation?',
 ['A gains about 1%; B loses about 2%, despite equal nominal rates','Both gain 6%, since account balances rise at the same rate','A loses 14%; B loses 34%, using basket-dollar increases as inflation','A gains about 11%; B gains about 14%, adding inflation to interest'],
 'Inflation is 20/400 = 5% for A and 40/500 = 8% for B. Subtracting from 6% gives +1% and -2%. The appropriate price growth, not the nominal rate alone, determines the purchasing-power return.','C/D: compare measured inflation across periods and interpret opposite real returns.', [('A inflation','(420/400-1)*100',5),('B inflation','(540/500-1)*100',8),('A real','6-5',1),('B real','6-8',-2)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9040'),'A lender wants a 2% approximate real return and forecasts CPI rising from 125 to 130. The resulting fixed nominal contract is signed, but CPI ends at 135. What nominal rate and realized real return follow?',
 ['6% nominal; -2% realized real return','7% nominal; -1% realized real return','6% nominal; 2% realized real return','10% nominal; 2% realized real return'],
 'Expected inflation is 5/125 = 4%, so the target nominal rate is 2 + 4 = 6%. Actual inflation is 10/125 = 8%, giving approximate realized return 6 - 8 = -2%. Use the stated approximation, not the exact Fisher ratio.','C/D: forecast-index inference, contract setting and realized real return.', [('nominal','2+(130/125-1)*100',6),('actual inflation','(135/125-1)*100',8),('real','6-8',-2)])
